    In my code:
    $S(\nu)$ is the sample. $R(\nu)$ is the reference.
    I used `signal.correlate(s_norm, r_norm)`.
    The lag is defined such that $\text{corr}[l] = \sum s[n] r[n-l]$.
    If the peak is at $l=3$, then $s[n]$ aligns with $r[n-3]$.
    This means $S$ has its peaks 3 units *after* (at higher wavenumbers) those of $R$.
    So Sample Peak $\approx$ Reference Peak + 3.
    To correct it: Corrected Sample = Sample - 3.
    Thus, a lag of $+3$ corresponds to the "positive shift" defined by the user.
